WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.419
Picture a newer colleague three months into the

2
00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:02.690
job.

3
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.760
They send a client letter with one typo.

4
00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.072
Within an hour it comes up in a

5
00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:08.955
team chat, in a reply-all and in a

6
00:00:08.955 --> 00:00:09.190
one-on-one.

7
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.580
Nobody is unkind.

8
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.530
So what did that colleague just learn?

9
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.420
That a typo is a big deal here.

10
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.710
Probably bigger than the policy says.

11
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.972
And that the safest move is to have

12
00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:21.864
three people check every routine letter from now

13
00:00:21.864 --> 00:00:22.100
on.

14
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:22.870
Right.

15
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.300
Nobody wrote that rule down.

16
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.170
The team taught it in an afternoon.

17
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.321
That is what culture is: the shared habits

18
00:00:29.321 --> 00:00:32.142
and assumptions that tell people what really matters,

19
00:00:32.142 --> 00:00:33.200
mostly without words.

20
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.410
But accuracy matters.

21
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.287
A wrong address on a notice can mean

22
00:00:38.287 --> 00:00:39.480
someone misses an appointment.

23
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:41.668
I don't want us to get casual about

24
00:00:41.668 --> 00:00:41.890
that.

25
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:43.100
Neither do I.

26
00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.272
The question isn't whether to keep a high

27
00:00:45.272 --> 00:00:45.490
standard.

28
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.700
It's whether the response matches the stakes.

29
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.990
A clear check for things that can hurt

30
00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:50.910
someone is a standard.

31
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.477
Treating every small slip as a crisis is

32
00:00:53.477 --> 00:00:55.080
a habit, and it has costs.

33
00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.430
Like the three reviewers for a routine letter.

34
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:02.240
That's time those reviewers aren't spending with families.

35
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:03.970
And something quieter.

36
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.200
When mistakes feel dangerous, people stop mentioning them.

37
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.050
Research on team learning has found that stronger

38
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.610
teams sometimes report more errors, not fewer, because

39
00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.850
people feel safe enough to say so.

40
00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.520
So the number of mistakes you hear about

41
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.580
isn't the same as the number that happen.

42
00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:20.650
Exactly.

43
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.440
Silence can look like quality from the outside.

44
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.275
Say a notice did go out with the

45
00:01:26.275 --> 00:01:28.381
wrong office address, and a client traveled to

46
00:01:28.381 --> 00:01:29.170
the old location.

47
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.660
What does a good response sound like?

48
00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.354
Start with the person affected: fix it for

49
00:01:34.354 --> 00:01:34.890
them first.

50
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.880
Then ask what about the process let it

51
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.160
happen, before asking who.

52
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.370
Was the address stored in three places?

53
00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.460
Did the template pull an old value?

54
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.808
And the person who sent it is in

55
00:01:46.808 --> 00:01:48.470
the room, probably feeling awful.

56
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.048
Which is why the supervisor goes first if

57
00:01:51.048 --> 00:01:51.540
they can.

58
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.867
Something like, here's what happened, here's what we've

59
00:01:53.867 --> 00:01:55.864
already fixed for the client, and here's the

60
00:01:55.864 --> 00:01:57.861
part of our process I want us to

61
00:01:57.861 --> 00:01:58.610
look at together.

62
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.640
Here's the harder one for me.

63
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.050
What if the mistake is the supervisor's?

64
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.537
A wrong deadline in the team update, and

65
00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:06.960
you're the newest person there.

66
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.330
Keep it short, private and about the work.

67
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.572
I think the report deadline in the update

68
00:02:11.572 --> 00:02:13.280
might be the 15th, not the 20th.

69
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.050
Want me to check?

70
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:14.760
That's it.

71
00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:15.970
No apology tour.

72
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.634
And if the supervisor thanks them for it,

73
00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:20.587
in front of the team, the next person

74
00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:21.320
speaks up faster.

75
00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.130
That's how a norm shifts.

76
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.220
Not with a poster.

77
00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.710
With what happens the next time someone says,

78
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.210
I think we got this wrong.

79
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.240
In the course, you'll practice telling standards from

80
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:33.640
habits, and responding to mistakes in a way

81
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.840
that keeps people talking.
